        "timestamp": 1776851787,
        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Displaced Palestinians return to books as Gaza's Phoenix library rises from the rubble Issued on: 22/04/2026 - 11:56 Displaced Palestinians browse books at Gaza's new Phoenix Library, a youth-led initiative in the Rimal neighbourhood that aims to revive culture and literature during the war. The library houses a diverse collection of literary and scientific books, and includes a children's section, its name reflecting Gaza's rise from the rubble. \"We retrieved the books from amidst the rubble many of which were in poor condition and proceeded to sort and clean them,\" says library owner Omar Hamad. 22/04/2026 Pope calls for 'law and justice' on Equatorial Guinea visit 22/04/2026 Trump extends ceasefire with Iran, says Tehran regime 'seriously fractured' 22/04/2026 ‘We cannot let Iran be our spokesperson’: Lebanon charts own path in talks with Israel 22/04/2026 Middle East IRGC asserting themselves as Iran 'responds to Trump’s method of conducting diplomacy in kind' 22/04/2026 Middle East Japan scraps a ban on lethal weapons exports in a change of its postwar pacifist policy 21/04/2026 Asia / Pacific 'Landmark' EU court ruling rejects Hungary anti-LGBTQ law 21/04/2026 Europe 21/04/2026 Middle East More than $71 billion needed over next decade to rebuild Gaza: UN, EU 21/04/2026 Middle East"
